steps.body:
An array type says what every element is. Write the element type followed by `[]`, as in `number[]`, or use `Array<number>`, which means exactly the same thing. Most code uses `number[]`.

```typescript
const prices: number[] = [4.5, 12, 3.25];
const names: Array<string> = ["Ana", "Ben"];
console.log(prices.length, names.length); // 3 2
```

From then on the checker holds each array to its type, so `prices.push` only takes numbers.

You rarely write the type yourself. TypeScript infers it from the elements, and a callback you pass to `map`, `filter`, or `reduce` gets its parameter type from the array, so `p` needs no annotation here:

```typescript
const prices = [4.5, 12, 3.25];
//    ^? const prices: number[]
const doubled = prices.map((p) => p * 2);
//                          ^? (parameter) p: number
console.log(doubled.join(", ")); // 9, 24, 6.5
```

An empty array has no elements to infer from, so annotate it: `const names: string[] = [];`.

examples.code:
const scores: number[] = [88, 92, 79];
scores.push(95);

const total = scores.reduce((sum, score) => sum + score, 0);
const average = total / scores.length;

console.log(`Scores: ${scores.join(", ")}`);
console.log(`Best: ${Math.max(...scores)}`);
console.log(`Average: ${average}`);

const tags = ["ts", "node", "zod"];
//    ^? const tags: string[]

const loud = tags.map((tag) => tag.toUpperCase());
//                     ^? (parameter) tag: string

console.log(loud.join(", "));
console.log(`${tags.length} tags: ${tags.join(" ")}`);

